<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stade</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stade is a town on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. A Hanseatic port and a residence of the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, it was from 1648 the seat of the Swedish government of the duchies of Bremen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a>. Its old harbour and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-framed houses survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stade.jpg" alt="Stade" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: © Ra Boe / Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luftaufnahmen_Nordseekueste_2012-05-by-RaBoe-485.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Stade lies in the lower reaches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 45 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, and is the seat of the district that bears its name. It is first mentioned in records in 934, and it lies on the German Timber-Frame Road. Settlers first came to the area in about 30,000 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Swedish and Danish Vikings under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-the-victorious">Eric the Victorious</a> conquered and looted Stade in the 990s and took many prominent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> back as slaves. From 1180 it belonged to the Prince-Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>. In 1208 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-ii-of-denmark">Valdemar II of Denmark</a> conquered it, and in the following years it changed hands several times. On 2 May 1209 Emperor Otto IV granted it important privileges: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> were confirmed as personally free and as a political entity under their own law, and property within the town could not be subjected to feudal overlordship.</p><p>After Danish attacks ended in 1216, Stade remained part of the Prince-Archbishopric. From the 13th century to the late 17th it was a prominent member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, and it was later eclipsed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> in 1648 turned the Prince-Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> into the Duchy of Bremen and granted it, with the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a>, to the Swedish crown. Stade was the capital of the Swedish province, and some buildings the Swedes built are still in use.</p><p>Two-thirds of the town were destroyed in a great fire on 26 May 1659 and rebuilt on the same plan. Allied forces held it from 1675 to 1679, and the Treaty of Saint Germain returned it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Swedish rule ended in 1712, when Danish troops shelled the town for ten days and destroyed 152 houses, a quarter of the built-up area. It stayed the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen-verden">Bremen-Verden</a> under the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> and of the Stade region after 1823.</p><p>The plague of 1355 and that of 1712 each killed at least 30 to 40 per cent of the city's population. In 1757, after the French invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-augustus-king-of-hanover">Duke of Cumberland</a> and the Hanoverian government took shelter in Stade. A Jewish community was permitted in 1611, and in 1845 a regional rabbinate served 16 congregations. Stade was untouched by Allied bombing in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The old town has notable buildings such as the St. Cosmae et Damiani and Wilhadi Lutheran churches, the city hall, the Schwedenspeicher and the Zeughaus. The decommissioned Stade Nuclear Power Plant on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> was connected to the grid in 1972 and closed in 2003.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stade” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Islamic State gunmen and suicide bombers attacked the Bataclan concert hall, cafés and the <b>Stade</b> de France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-13">13 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Sweden governed the duchies from <b>Stade</b>, which it fortified, through a governor-general and the local estates.</blockquote><a href="/state/bremen-verden">Bremen-Verden</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Peace of Westphalia in 1648 they were secularised and granted to the Swedish crown as the duchies of Bremen and Verden, with the government seated at <b>Stade</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchies-of-bremen-and-verden">Duchies of Bremen and Verden</a></li><li><blockquote>The archbishops governed marshland and moor from <b>Stade</b> and Bremervörde, while the wealthy city of Bremen slipped from their control.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-archbishopric-of-bremen">Prince-Archbishopric of Bremen</a></li></ul></section>
